# Developer workflow

How to work with AI coding agents in this project. **Not a process** — a toolkit
of independent skills you compose as the task demands.

## Philosophy

A workflow is not a checklist; it is a vocabulary. Each of the nine skills in
`skills/` has a clear **trigger** (when), defined **input** (what goes in) and
defined **output** (what comes out). You combine and skip as the work calls for.

The point is not to follow every step — it is to know which step the situation
needs.

## Two task sizes

**Small tasks** (under ~2 hours, known codebase, you're at the wheel):

```
/explore (if needed)  →  /plan  →  implement  →  /review
```

No formal spec, no autonomous delegation. The overhead would exceed the benefit.

**Big tasks** (a real feature, unfamiliar codebase, or autonomous agent run):

```
/explore → /spec → /plan → /test → /delegate → /review → /verify → /capture
```

Not every step is always necessary. Your judgement decides. The workflow doesn't
force — it structures.

> **Rule of thumb:** if you review every commit yourself and don't let an agent
> run unattended, you don't need the full flow.

## Three operating modes

| Mode | Who codes | When |
|---|---|---|
| **Solo** | You implement; the AI is a sparring partner and reviewer. | Deep refactors where you want full control. |
| **Hybrid** | You plan and review; the agent implements under your eye. | The everyday mode this project is tuned for. |
| **Delegated** | You hand off a full spec and plan; the agent runs autonomously to review. | Unattended runs in an isolated sandbox, picked up afterwards for review. |

## The nine skills

| Skill | Trigger | Output |
|---|---|---|
| [`/explore`](../skills/explore/SKILL.md) | "I need to understand this area" | Relevant files, patterns, open questions |
| [`/spec`](../skills/spec/SKILL.md) | "I need to define a feature" | Requirements, acceptance criteria, edge cases, out-of-scope |
| [`/plan`](../skills/plan/SKILL.md) | "How do I approach this?" | Ordered steps with dependencies + verification |
| [`/test`](../skills/test/SKILL.md) | "I need tests from this spec" | Pytest functions mapped to acceptance criteria |
| [`/delegate`](../skills/delegate/SKILL.md) | "An agent should run this autonomously" | Self-contained brief: spec + plan + tests + constraints |
| [`/review`](../skills/review/SKILL.md) | "I want to check this change" | Findings by severity, verdict |
| [`/verify`](../skills/verify/SKILL.md) | "Prove the change works before saying done" | Evidence (command + output) mapped to acceptance criteria |
| [`/debug`](../skills/debug/SKILL.md) | "Something doesn't work" | Repro, hypotheses, root cause, fix + regression test |
| [`/capture`](../skills/capture/SKILL.md) | "I learned something" | One-line dated rule with *why* |

## How the skills compose

`/plan` replaces `/spec` for small tasks and **decomposes** a spec for big
tasks — no separate breakdown step.

`/delegate` consumes the output of `/spec` and `/plan`. Without that foundation
the receiving agent fills the gaps with guesswork.

`/capture` is **not** an appendix to `/review`. It is a separate step, because
otherwise learnings die in the chat window.

## Feedback loops

While writing a `/spec`, a knowledge gap may force you back to `/explore`. That
is not a failure — it is the workflow doing its job.

If an agent during implementation discovers the spec doesn't cover a case:
**stop, return to `/spec`, do not improvise**.

## Three orthogonal axes

The workflow lives on three independent axes. You only need to actively
remember the first two:

- **Skills (the WHAT):** the 9 verbs above. Single source of truth, tool-agnostic.
- **Modes (the WHERE):** typically two — an architect seat (read-only, planning)
  and a coder seat (implementation). In Claude Code these are *Plan Mode* +
  *Default*; in OpenCode the `plan` and `build` primary modes; in Codex, Pi,
  and Aider the same separation can live in the conversation.
- **Subagents (the HOW):** where the selected runtime supports them, read-only
  specialists (`researcher`, `reviewer`, `security-auditor`) can isolate recon
  and review. Pi needs an extension for subagents; Aider has no configured
  subagent surface in this template.

## Where to start

For your first real task on this project: run `/explore` to map the area, then
`/plan` your approach, then go. Reach for `/spec` and `/delegate` only when the
scope justifies them.
